===Etymology 1===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:tiren|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|tiren}}, {{m|enm|tirien}}, {{m|enm|teorien}}, from {{inh|en|ang|tȳrian}}, {{m|ang|tēorian|t=to fail, cease, become weary, be tired, exhausted; tire, weary, exhaust}}, of uncertain origin. Possibly from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*teuʀōn|t=to cease}}, which is possibly from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dews-|t=to fail, be behind, lag}}. Compare {{cog|grc|δεύομαι|t=to lack}}, {{cog|sa|दोष|tr=dóṣa|t=crime, fault, vice, deficiency}}.<ref>J.P. Mallory & D.Q. Adams, ''Encyclopedia of Indo-European Culture'', s.v. "lack: deu(s)-" (London: Fitzroy-Dearborn, 1997), 343.</ref>

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|tyre||dialectal}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[sleepy]] or [[weary]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make [[sleepy]] or [[weary]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:tire}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[bored]] or [[impatient]] (with).
#: {{ux|en|I '''tire''' of this book.}}
# {{senseid|en|bore}}{{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bore]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:cause boredom}}

