===Etymology 2===
Probably from {{inh|en|enm|tir|tir(e)|t=equipment, furnshings, ornament}}. See the details at {{m|en|tyre}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{sense|rubber covering on a wheel}} {{alter|en|tyre}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{alternative spelling of|en|tyre#Etymology 1|from=US|from2=Canada}}: The rubber covering on a wheel.
# {{alternative spelling of|en|tyre#Etymology 1|from=US}}: The metal rim of a wheel, especially that of a [[railroad]] [[locomotive]].
# A child's [[apron]] covering the upper part of the body, and tied with tape or cord; a [[pinafore]]. Also {{m|en|tier}}.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[accoutrements|Accoutrements]], [[accessories]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1705|author=w:John Philips|title=Blenheim
|passage=the '''tire''' of war}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[dress|Dress]], [[clothes]], [[attire]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|I|vii|passage= Ne spared they to strip her naked all. / Then when they had despoild her '''tire''' and call, / Such as she was, their eyes might her behold.}}
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2001|page=66|passage=men like apes follow the fashions in '''tires''', gestures, actions: if the king laugh, all laugh{{nb...}}.}}
# A covering for the head; a headdress.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|I|x|page=144|passage=And on her head she wore a '''tyre''' of gold,}}

=====Usage notes=====
* ''Tire'' is one of the few words where Canadian usage prefers the US spelling over the British spelling.

