===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|tire}}, from {{der|en|fro|tirer|t=to draw or pull}}, akin to {{cog|en|tear|t=to rend}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|tyre}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To seize, pull, and tear prey, as a hawk does.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Venus and Adonis|text=Even as an empty eagle, sharp by fast, / '''Tires''' with her beak on feathers, flesh, and bone.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Cymbeline|III|iv|line=94–97|text=I grieve myself / To think, when thou shalt be disedged by her / That now thou '''tirest''' on, how thy memory / Will then be pang'd by me.}}
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|The Honest Man's Fortune|2|5|passage=Ye dregs of baseness, vultures amongst men, / That '''tire''' upon the hearts of generous spirits.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To seize, rend, or tear something as prey; to be fixed upon, or engaged with, anything.
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=vii|page=41|passage=and now doth gaſtly death<br>With greedie talients gripe my bleeding hart,<br>And like a Harpye '''tires''' on my life.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1616|author=w:George Chapman|title=Iliad
|passage=Thus made she her remove, / And left wrath '''tyring''' on her son.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Timon of Athens|text=Upon that were my thoughts '''tiring'''.}}

